many nodes you need.

00:15:10.027 --> 00:15:11.360
You'll give us some information.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.880
And then we'll
automatically provision them

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.480
and give you access to them.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.540
And we'll show that in the demo.

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.850
These are the three
t-shirt sizes for Oracle

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:23.070
that we're offering standard
through marketplace.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.120
And then, we also have
smaller virtual Oracle servers

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:29.030
starting at 2 cores
and go up to 16 cores.

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:34.130
This uses OVM, again, so that
you can honor that EULA license

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:36.350
for the core cost.

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:39.710
And the operating system for
all of the Oracle environments

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:42.270
are either Red Hat Enterprise
Linux or Oracle Enterprise

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:42.770
Linux.

00:15:42.770 --> 00:15:45.560
Those are the two options
for operating systems.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:47.030
Again, because it's--

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:50.810
I keep saying again, excuse
me-- because it's bare-metal,

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.480
you do have an option if you
have an older version of Oracle

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.855
that's maybe not supported--
we talked to a customer that

00:15:55.855 --> 00:15:59.070
had Oracle Version 9 or you want
a different operating system--

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:01.640
we are able to give
you a bare-metal box.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:04.820
And then you will configure
that box to meet your needs.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.250
At that point, some of our
support standard services

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:09.860
are on backup and monitoring no
longer in effect because it's

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.300
a non-standard deployment.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.210
But it still enables you
to get some of those older

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:16.860
environments and
workloads running into GCP

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:17.625
and connect there.

00:16:17.625 --> 00:16:19.250
You still get all
the network benefits.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:20.843
You still get all
the connectivity.

00:16:20.843 --> 00:16:22.760
It's just that some of
our management services

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:28.650
will scale back a little bit
because of diversion control.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:31.970
And then finally, if you
have individual VM guests.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.370
Again, this is really
meant for if you've

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:38.060
got an app or a web tier
