00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:38.670
that's running on the
AOPC connected into GCP.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:41.070
And we said it's
a fast connection.

00:27:41.070 --> 00:27:47.440
If we switch to the ping, so
we're pinging that same IP

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:51.090
address 10.200.0.22.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.200
And you can see that--

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:54.760
well, I should
look at the screen.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:56.650
It is 1 millisecond away.

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:59.830
So this is still going
through the RACK network.

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:02.530
So it goes up to the top of
RACK, goes through a firewall

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:05.140
that we have, through
the segregated VPC,

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:09.100
back into GCP hitting a VM
within should be less than 1

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:09.610
millisecond.

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:11.920
I think ping doesn't
really show that.

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:15.040
But at most, it's 1 millisecond.

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:18.430
So that should enable you to run
most of your application tier

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:22.270
or your web tier in GCP and
still get good performance back

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:24.160
to hit that database.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:26.480
As I said earlier,
there's no data egress,

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:31.260
so you can suck all that
data into BigQuery, BigTable,

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:34.810
run ML/AI against it, any
of the cool data stuff

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:38.265
